  "@type": "Process",
  "critical": false,
  "description": "Integration of a hot-hydrogen convergent-divergent nozzle with the reactor core exit: hot hydrogen at 2500-3000 K expands through a refractory or regeneratively-cooled nozzle to produce thrust at 850-1000 s Isp. The nozzle is underexpanded for vacuum operation (high expansion ratio 80:1 to 300:1), distinguishing it from sea-level chemical nozzles. Nozzle materials must survive flowing hot hydrogen without erosion: graphite, carbon-carbon composites, or regeneratively-cooled copper alloys with LH2 circulated through channels. The nozzle is the principal Isp-setting component: expansion ratio and exit Mach number set the specific impulse delivered for a given core exit temperature.",
  "early_win": false,
